Since there's no project file to check the target framework, here are the two lightest options — no DI container needed:

---

### Option 1 — .NET 8+: Use the built-in `TimeProvider`

`TimeProvider` is an abstract class already in the BCL. Add a defaulted parameter or a static ambient:

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _clock;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? clock = null)
        => _clock = clock ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _clock.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

In tests, use `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider`:

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 6, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

### Option 2 — Older TFMs: A tiny `IClock` interface

```csharp
public interface IClock { DateTimeOffset UtcNow { get; } }

public sealed class SystemClock : IClock
{
    public static readonly SystemClock Instance = new();
    public DateTimeOffset UtcNow => DateTimeOffset.UtcNow;
}
```

Then the same defaulted-parameter pattern:

```csharp
public InvoiceCalculator(IClock? clock = null)
    => _clock = clock ?? SystemClock.Instance;
```

---

**Recommendation:** If you're on .NET 8+, go with `TimeProvider` — zero new interfaces, first-party support, and `FakeTimeProvider` ships in the `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` NuGet package. Want me to apply one of these to your code?
